<misterromdescription>
    <name>E Jong High School (Japan)</name>
    <setname>ejanhs</setname>
    <rbf>SeibuSPI</rbf>
    <mameversion>0277</mameversion>
    <year>1996</year>
    <manufacturer>Seibu Kaihatsu</manufacturer>
    <players>1</players>
    <joystick>8</joystick>
    <rotation>horizontal</rotation>
    <region>Japan</region>
    <category>Mahjong</category>

    <!--
      CONTROLS DO NOT WORK. E-Jan High School is a mahjong game and reads its
      panel through a custom encoding (MAME's ejanhs_encode packs the key
      matrix into three-bit fields), which this core does not decode - it
      wires the standard SPI joystick ports every other set uses. The flash
      copy, the video and the sound all work; playing it does not. The MRA is
      here because the set otherwise runs, and because the input work is a
      separate job from the one this file is part of.
    -->

    <!--
      THE SAMPLE FLASH. The SXX2C cartridge has no sample ROM: the YMF271 reads
      two flash chips the game programs itself, and this MRA ships them BLANK
      along with the ROMs the game's updater reads to fill them. What fills
      them is an OSD option, "Sample Flash":

      BOTH settings derive the 2 MB payload out of SDRAM in about a third of a
      second. What the option picks is whether the region STAMP goes with it,
      because that is the only thing the game tests:

        Pre-built (default)  Payload and stamp. The game finds a programmed
                             flash and plays at once. Same job table, same
                             sources, same bytes as the game's own updater -
                             checked against the image MAME's flash devices
                             hold, on all seven sets.

        Cart copy            Payload only. The game finds a blank stamp and
                             spends about six minutes programming a flash whose
                             contents are already correct, the way the real
                             cartridge does at first boot. It ends on "UPDATE
                             COMPLETED. PLEASE TURN THE POWER BACK ON".

                             SWITCHING to it blanks the stamp and restarts the
                             board, because boot is the only moment the game
                             looks - so the copy can always be seen again.
                             Switching back does nothing: Pre-built writes the
                             real stamp at the next boot anyway.

      Pre-built ignores the stamp in the save file, deliberately: it writes the
      real one from the program image a moment later, so a stored copy could only
      be the same bytes or a stale set. Cart copy uses it, and that is the whole
      mechanism - a restored stamp means the copy has been done and the game
      skips its updater. The DS2404's 512-byte tail is live either way.
    -->

    <!--
      Part order is significant. rtl/rom_loader.sv walks this list with a fixed
      table - selected by the mod byte below - and performs MAME's
      ROM_LOAD24_* / ROM_LOAD32_* byte scatter in hardware. `make check-mra`
      holds this file, that table and MAME's ROM_START against each other.

      This is a GENERATION A cartridge, which differs from rdft's family in two
      ways the core cares about:

        * the program is 1 MB in the UPPER half of the 2 MB region - four
          256 KB ROMs at offset 0x100000 - so the first part is a megabyte of
          zeroes for the half MAME leaves empty. Nothing is known to read it;
          filling it means the region matches MAME's rather than holding
          whatever the previously loaded core left in SDRAM.
        * the PCM source is 1 MB on ONE byte lane where rdft's is 2 MB on two.
          spi_cpu.sv's pcmsrc_1lane is that bit.

      Decryption is the same SEI252 as rdft (MAME's init is init_sei252), so
      nothing in the graphics path is new.
    -->

    <!--
      Index 1 is the core's config blob. Byte 0 is the mod byte: bit 0 selects
      the SXX2C board wiring, bits 3:1 the set, and BIT 4 the authentic-flash
      tail plus the 386's sound01 source window.

      IT MUST COME BEFORE THE index="0" ROM.
    -->
    <rom index="1">
      <!--
        Byte 0 is the mod byte. Bytes 1-15 are the {part index, codec}
        pairs, all FF here because this variant decodes nothing on the
        way in - FF is the documented terminator.

        Bytes 16-24 are the sample-flash derivation's constants, read
        at fixed offsets by SeibuSPI.sv. They are in the MRA and not in
        the RTL because a CLONE has its own job-table address: its
        program differs, so the table moves. senkyu is 0x00302324 and
        batlball 0x00302290, same game, same graphics, same everything
        else. In the RTL that would be a core rebuild per clone.

          job table  0x003026ac
          stamp      0x003ffffc   written to flash[0..3] LAST
          generation 0            A

        All three come out of tools/build_soundflash.py's GAMES table,
        which read them out of the game's own program image; make
        check-mra holds this block against it.
      -->
      <part>1B</part>
      <part>FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF</part>
      <part>AC 26 30 00</part>
      <part>FC FF 3F 00</part>
      <part>00</part>
    </rom>

    <rom index="0" address="0x30000000" zip="ejanhs.zip" md5="none">
        <!-- the empty lower half of the program region -->
        <part repeat="0x100000">00</part>

        <!-- 386 program: four 256 KB byte lanes at region offset 0x100000 -->
        <part name="ejan3_1.211" crc="e626d3d2"/>
        <part name="ejan3_2.212" crc="83c39da2"/>
        <part name="ejan3_3.210" crc="46897b7d"/>
        <part name="ejan3_4.29"  crc="b3187a2b"/>

        <!-- text layer: a word plus a byte -->
        <part name="ejan3_6.413" crc="837e012c"/>
        <part name="ejan3_5.48"  crc="d62db7bf"/>

        <!-- background tiles: 6 MB, the second group half the size -->
        <part name="ej3_bg1d.415" crc="bcacabe0"/>
        <part name="ej3_bg1p.410" crc="1fd0eb5e"/>
        <part name="ej3_bg2d.416" crc="ea2acd69"/>
        <part name="ej3_bg2p.49"  crc="a4a9cb0f"/>

        <!-- sprites: 12 MB in three 4 MB plane-pair chunks, SEI252 -->
        <part name="ej3_obj1.322" crc="852f180e"/>
        <part name="ej3_obj2.324" crc="1116ad08"/>
        <part name="ej3_obj3.323" crc="ccfe02b6"/>

        <!-- MAME's sound01: the PCM source, then the second sound ROM -->
        <part name="ej3_pcm1.215" crc="a92a3a82"/>
        <part name="ejan3_7.216"  crc="c6fc6bcf"/>

        <!--
          The sample flash, BLANK. flash0_blank_region01.u1053 is a real dump
          and carries the region ID in byte 0 - 01, Japan, for this set. MAME
          has no dump for the second E28F008SA because an erased chip has
          nothing to dump, so it is a megabyte of FF here.
        -->
        <part name="flash0_blank_region01.u1053" crc="7ae7ab76"/>
        <part repeat="0x100000">FF</part>
    </rom>

    <!--
      The save file, in /media/fat/config/nvram/. Index 2 because 0 is the ROM
      image, 1 the core's config blob and 254 the DIP switches.

      ONE element, because an MRA gets one, so what the board remembers is
      concatenated into it - and it is 516 bytes, not two megabytes:

        0x000..0x003   the sample flash's REGION STAMP, and nothing else of it
        0x004..0x203   the DS2404's 512 bytes of bookkeeping SRAM

      Those four bytes are what the game tests to decide the flash is already
      programmed, so they are the whole flag that says whether its own updater
      runs. The two megabytes behind them are DERIVED at every boot in a third of
      a second, which made storing them pointless twice over - and expensive:
      Main reads the save file back every time it polls, so a 2 MB one made the
      OSD visibly unresponsive on every open.

      The stamp comes first so that the tail is what moves on a set with no flash
      at all: SXX2E's samples are a real ROM, so its file is those 512 bytes and
      nothing else. The tail is byte-for-byte MAME's own `ds2404` nvram file.

      Main_MiSTer sends this file AFTER the index-0 image, which is what lets it
      land on top of the blank flash that image carries, so it must stay below
      the <rom> element here.
    -->
    <nvram index="2" size="516"/>

    <!--
      Same control layout as the other sets; the names map to joystick bits
      4 upwards IN ORDER and SeibuSPI.sv decodes them at exactly those positions.
      The last two are not board inputs: Pause (bit 11) gates the 386, and
      Savestate (bit 12) is the modifier for the savestate d-pad combinations.
      Savestate has no default= entry and must be bound by hand.
    -->
    <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,Button 3,Start,Coin,Service Coin,Test,Pause,Savestate (SS)"
             default="A,B,X,Start,Select,R,L,Y" count="3"/>

    <switches default="00" base="0">
        <dip name="Flip Screen"  bits="0" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="1" ids="Off,On"/>
    </switches>
</misterromdescription>
